<p>As a skill for making skills, it comes with the following flow and subagents.</p>
<ul>
    <li>A <strong>create → evaluate → improve → benchmark</strong> loop.</li>
    <li><strong>evaluator, grader, comparison, and analysis</strong> subagents built in.</li>
    <li>Drop in a script and say "turn this into a skill," and it builds a solid one through <strong>its own loop and testing (A/B)</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Two categories</h4>
<div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What it covers</th><th>As models advance</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Capability-boosting skills</strong></td><td>PDF manipulation, document generation, complex file conversion, and the like. Built as scripts so they run <strong>deterministically and reliably</strong>.</td><td>Once the model gets good at it, they <strong>may become unnecessary</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Organization-internalizing skills</strong></td><td>The organization's responsibilities, workflows, owners, and so on. <strong>Our internal knowledge</strong>, which the agent does not know.</td><td><strong>Irreplaceable</strong> no matter how much models advance &mdash; keep building them</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Emphasis &mdash; keep building organization-internalizing skills</span>
    Make good use of capability-boosting skills now, but do not assume they will "last forever" &mdash; view them as something you can retire as models advance. <strong>Organization-internalizing skills</strong>, on the other hand, cannot be replaced no matter how far LLMs advance, because they cannot stand in for our organization's responsibilities, flows, and owners, so <strong>building them continuously</strong> is the heart of agent governance.
</div>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Distribution is easy</span>
    One of the biggest advantages of skills is <strong>distribution</strong>. Register one in a shared repository, server, or community and version management and sharing become easy: copy "Get this skill" → say "install it" once, and it spreads across the whole organization. Editing the file on the spot and redistributing it is possible immediately too.
</div>

<h3>Wrap-up &mdash; Engineering Layers and Q&A</h3>

<h4>The layers of engineering</h4>
<p>Sort what we have learned so far by purpose and, instead of "it's all engineering," the distinct grain of each becomes visible.</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Engineering</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Prompt engineering</td><td>Making the LLM <strong>smart</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Context engineering</td><td><strong>Raising the odds</strong> that the context is right</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Agentic engineering</td><td>Making the AI <strong>do the work well</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Harness engineering</td><td>Making it something you can <strong>trust and delegate to</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>A <a href="#!hns-overview">harness</a> can be seen as a <strong>meta skill loaded with skills + subagents</strong>. With the concept of skills in hand, you can look at a harness understanding that it is complex inside even though it looks easy on the surface.</p>

<h4>MCP vs skills</h4>
<p>MCP is a <strong>protocol</strong> &mdash; a convention for connecting external tools with agents, so its loading and usage differ from skills. <strong>They are not competitors</strong>: if a skill needs an external connection it can use MCP, so MCP can live inside a skill. That said, skills are so powerful that cases of <strong>replacing MCP with skills</strong> are increasing too.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Q&amp;A takeaways</span>
    · Downloading <strong>open skills</strong> carelessly is not safe &mdash; only vetted ones.<br>
    · When there is <strong>an MCP function and a skill with the same name</strong>, which one wins depends on the situation, so <strong>be explicit</strong> ("use this skill") or separate them with namespaces.<br>
    · <strong>Evaluating skill performance</strong> &mdash; decide metrics first, such as whether it triggers well and whether the result is useful, then check with an LLM judge and by eye.<br>
    · <strong>Tokens</strong> &mdash; Claude Code uses more than regular Claude, but it can be more useful if it gets you the result you want in one go. Do not look at tokens alone; choose holistically.
</div>
